<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Central package versions. Add PackageReference in csproj without Version.
       Pins follow the shared .NET toolchain. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Npgsql" Version="10.0.3" />
    <!-- libsodium wrapper (span-based, AOT-friendly): CSPRNG + hashing for
         secret/ticket material. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Geralt" Version="4.4.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- Trimmed MimeKit/MailKit forks (no legacy charset tables), used by
         libs/nativeweb.mail for SMTP send + templating. Pins mirror epass.io. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MailKitLite" Version="4.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MimeKitLite" Version="4.17.0" />
    <!-- JauntyQ pin: used only when building without a local jauntyq checkout
         (package mode, e.g. CI, -p:JauntyQUsePackages=true). Bump deliberately
         after a new jauntyq v* tag publishes.

         Extrode.JauntyQ.*, not JauntyQ.*: core 0.5.0 (2026-09-17) renamed every
         package id, assembly and namespace, and generated code moved from
         JauntyQ.Generated to Extrode.JauntyQ.Generated. The core is public on
         nuget.org from 0.5.0, so these resolve from nuget.org, the only source
         nuget.config declares, with no feed credential. The old JauntyQ.* ids were deleted
         from the extrode GitHub Packages feed, which is what turned every CI
         restore red: a warm ~/.nuget/packages hid it locally. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Extrode.JauntyQ.Generator" Version="0.5.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Extrode.JauntyQ.Runtime" Version="0.5.1" />
    <!-- Fido2 4.0.1 and NSec.Cryptography 26.4.0 were pinned here and were removed
         on 2026-08-31: WebAuthn left with the identity provider
         (docs/decisions/2026-08-31-007-idp-leaves-nativeweb.md) and neither had a
         PackageReference anywhere afterwards. Anything reintroducing Fido2 needs the
         NSec pin back with it: Fido2 floats a minimum on NSec 25.4.0, whose libsodium
         range [1.0.20.1, 1.0.21) conflicts with Geralt's exact [1.0.22, 1.0.23) pin
         and fails restore with NU1107. NSec 26.4.0 shares Geralt's range, which is what
         resolved both to one native asset. -->
    <!-- Telemetry (spec 003 R33): metrics + traces, exported over OTLP, gated
         behind NativeWeb:Telemetry:Enabled=false. All four move in
         lockstep; bump together. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Runtime" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.18.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Roslyn, for the four netstandard2.0 analyzer projects under libs/.
       Full reasoning and every measurement:
       docs/decisions/2026-09-05-011-roslyn-pin-raised-to-5-3.md, which
       supersedes 010. The short version:

       THE TWO LINES DO NOT MOVE TOGETHER. CSharp has a ceiling and a floor;
       Analyzers has neither.

       An analyzer referencing a NEWER Microsoft.CodeAnalysis than the compiler
       loading it is refused with CS9057. That one rule, at two compilers:

         ceiling  our SDK 10.0.203 runs Roslyn 5.3.0, so 5.9.0 cannot be used
         floor    whatever these compile against is the OLDEST Roslyn a
                  consumer of the four published packages may run

       Measured 2026-09-05, generator pin against consumer SDK:

         built against 4.12.0   SDK 9.0.313 (Roslyn 4.14.0)  runs
                                SDK 10.0.203 (Roslyn 5.3.0)  runs
         built against 5.3.0    SDK 9.0.313                  CS9057 + CS0103
                                SDK 10.0.203                 runs
         built against 5.9.0    SDK 10.0.203                 CS9057

       5.3.0 is the ceiling, so it is also the highest this can go today. It was
       4.12.0 until 2026-09-05, held there by justsmtp.com - the one consumer of
       the four published packages, and its global.json had no sdk section, so it
       built on whatever dotnet resolved. It pins 10.0.100 / latestFeature now,
       and no other repository consumes these packages: epass.io project-
       references nativeweb.render and nativeweb.security, neither of which
       references a generator.

       So the floor is a property of the CONSUMER SET, and it has to be
       re-checked whenever that set grows. Raise the pin when a generator here
       needs a newer API AND our SDK will load it, or when the consumer set
       clears a higher floor - NOT when this repository's SDK moves, which only
       lifts the ceiling.

       CS9057 is a WARNING, emitted by the CONSUMING project's csc, never by
       these four. It always means the generator did not run; what the consumer
       sees depends on their settings, and only the first is legible:

         TreatWarningsAsErrors on   error CS9057, naming the assembly
         off, output unused         warning, green build, generator inert
         off, output consumed       warning, plus CS0103 for every generated
                                    symbol, pointing at their own source

       This repository only ever sees the first, because Directory.Build.props
       sets TreatWarningsAsErrors repo-wide. That setting is the guard here: a
       generator that stops loading fails the build by name.

       Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers is exempt from both bounds. It is
       PrivateAssets=all, build-time only, never in a consumer's closure, and it
       is the RS* rules an analyzer author is checked against rather than a
       compiler API surface. Its one constraint is a restore-time floor dragged
       in by the CSharp pin, quoted from the NU1605 text: CSharp 4.12.0 requires
       >= 3.3.4, CSharp 5.3.0 requires >= 5.3.0-2.25625.1. That is why the two
       lines can only ever move up together, never down. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="5.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" Version="5.9.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Test-only packages. Referenced solely by tests/*, never by shipping code. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- R13 well-formedness gate: strict-mode HTML parsing of rendered template
         output in the render engine's unit tests. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AngleSharp" Version="1.7.3" />
    <!-- xUnit v3, which is Microsoft.Testing.Platform rather than VSTest: the v3
         packages depend on Microsoft.Testing.Platform and the .NET 10 SDK refuses
         to drive them through the VSTest target at all. So there is no
         Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and no xunit.runner.visualstudio pin here any more
         - both were dropped rather than bumped. Each test project sets
         OutputType=Exe (v3 errors without it) and `dotnet test` reaches them via
         the opt-in in global.json. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />
    <!-- The runner-less half of the same release, for a project that carries
         [Fact]s a CONSUMER runs rather than running them itself:
         libs/nativeweb.signin.testing. Referencing xunit.v3 there would demand an
         executable, which a packable library of abstract conformance suites
         cannot be. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="4.0.0" />
    <!-- Assert on its own, for the same project: the conformance suites assert,
         and extensibility.core carries the attributes and interfaces only. Both
         are plain libraries with no runner, which is the point. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.assert" Version="4.0.0" />
    <!-- Integration tests: WebApplicationFactory host. Matched to the AspNetCore
         10.0.x line (no sibling pin exists to mirror). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- The in-memory server behind tests/nativeweb.web.tests.unit (spec 015 T1).
         Mvc.Testing would drag WebApplicationFactory and an entry-point assembly
         in for a tier that composes its own pipeline; TestHost is the piece of it
         that matters here. Same 10.0.x line as the sibling pin above. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- Property-based testing (spec 018). Generators plus shrinking, for the
         paging seam and the cursor codec: the 017 defects each lived in a
         combination of row count, null density, page size and cursor state that
         no example test enumerated, and a mutation run confirmed the gap before
         this was added. CsCheck rather than FsCheck because it is a plain C#
         API with no FSharp.Core dependency. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="CsCheck" Version="4.8.0" />
    <!-- End-to-end browser tests. Net-new to this repo (no sibling reference);
         pinned to a recent 1.x. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Deque.AxeCore.Playwright" Version="4.12.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.62.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
